# ki_processing_failurs.log

Failed solution approaches and problems, with timestamps, to avoid repeating them.

- 2026-08-28T21:10Z — auto-smooth-follow: first `:auto:compileDebugKotlin` failed with
  `Unresolved reference 'isActive'` in `AutoMapRenderer.startExtrapolationLoop`.
  Cause: `kotlinx.coroutines.isActive` (CoroutineScope extension) was not imported.
  Fix: added the import. Lesson: any `while (isActive)` loop in a coroutine needs
  the explicit `kotlinx.coroutines.isActive` import — it is not auto-imported.
- 2026-08-29T09:10Z — auto-smooth-follow: `reengageFollow()` initially did NOT anchor
  viewport/emit viewportState. FreeDrivingScreen/NavigationScreen do per-fix
  `setViewport(vp.lat,vp.lon)` where vp comes from viewportState; without the
  anchor, viewportState stayed at the initial center and every per-fix render
  targeted the stale center, then `fullRender` reset the eased display there ->
  visible vehicle "pumping" (display oscillating between stale center and fix).
  Fix: `reengageFollow` anchors viewport=fix + emits, and `fullRender`/blit
  only set display=viewport when !followMode (the extrapolation loop owns the
  display in follow mode). Lesson: whenever a screen re-engages follow after a
  transient setViewport, the render target must follow the fix, and renders
  must never clobber the loop-owned display state.
- 2026-08-29T09:10Z — auto-smooth-follow: initial implementation gated the
  extrapolation loop off when the fix was older than 3 s. During the emulator
  GPX's 14.4 s gap the display froze ~46 m ahead of the fix (the phone instead
  lets FollowPrediction hold the position and eases the display back). Fix:
  removed the staleness gate; FollowPrediction.predictedPosition already holds
  past its extrapolation window. Lesson: a stale fix must ease the display back
  to the last fix, never freeze the prediction position.
- 2026-08-29T09:10Z — auto-smooth-follow: used EASE_TAU_SEC=0.1 (design text
  said tau~100 ms) while the phone uses the 0.3 s default; the shorter tau made
  the display run further ahead of the fix and the fix-arrival correction jerk
  back (overshoot). Fix: EASE_TAU_SEC=0.3 and MOVEMENT_SPEED_MS=0.5 (1.8 km/h)
  to match the phone's constants exactly.
- 2026-08-29T16:35Z — continuous-pinch-zoom: emulator crash `UnsatisfiedLinkError:
  dlopen failed: library "libosmscout_client_javad.so" not found`. Cause: rebuilt
  APK with `-Pandroid.injected.build.abi=arm64-v8a` for a fast check, then installed
  that APK on the x86_64 emulator — Gradle incremental packaging STRIPPED the
  x86_64 .so set from the APK (only arm64 present) while the install still
  succeeded. Fix: always rebuild full-ABI (`./gradlew :app:assembleMobileDebug`)
  before installing on the emulator; verify with `unzip -l ...apk | grep x86_64`.
  Lesson: ABI-filtered builds produce an APK that installs fine on mismatched
  emulators but crashes at loadLibrary — never install ABI-filtered output.
- 2026-08-29T16:35Z — continuous-pinch-zoom: `FakeOSMScoutClient.lastRenderMag`
  trap — after moving the JNI render call to the scale factor (2^z), the fake
  records the RAW scale (4096 at level 12), so tests comparing `lastRenderMag`
  against a level number time out. Fix: tests compare `2.0.pow(level)` (see
  MiniMapComposeTest.awaitMag). Lesson: after any boundary signature semantic
  change, grep tests for the boundary-observable fields (`lastRenderMag`) first.
- 2026-08-29T16:35Z — continuous-pinch-zoom: hand-written test expectations for
  log2 arithmetic three times wrong (2·√2 ≈ 2.828 → +1.5 levels not 14.5 from
  14.0; log2(0.4) = 1.3219 → 14.678 from 16.0). Lesson: compute log2 expected
  values with a calculator, not mentally.
- 2026-08-29T16:45Z — continuous-pinch-zoom: "pinch jumpy + smaller map rendered
  in top-left rectangle" on device (user report). Two independent causes:
  (1) TILE PATH drew floor-level tiles at natural size (drawBitmap(tile, px, py))
      while the fractional viewport projection spaces them 2^(z−floor(z))×
      further apart — seams + shrunken repeated tile content = "smaller map
      rectangles". Fix: compose tiles into a scaled destination RectF
      (tileSizePx × 2^(z−floor(z))) in both rotated and north-up paths.
  (2) Double-applied display scale: a pinch over a parked/frozen smooth-zoom
      scale kept zoomAnimScale ≠ 1 in the draw layer while the pinch's
      graphicsLayer added gestureZoom on top → oversized ghost map + jumpy
      double transform; render-land then crossfaded with stale scale/anchor.
      Fix: one displayed-scale model — freezeZoomAnimationForGesture folds the
      frozen zoomAnimScale into gestureBaseScale (gestureZoom = base × factor,
      draw-layer scale resets to 1f), reset on gesture end; commit uses the
      detector factor only (base already committed in viewport.magnification).
  Lesson: two independent display transforms (draw-layer scale × graphicsLayer
  scale) must never both be live; fold at the handover point. And any render
  path that composes cached integer-level content must scale dest rects when
  the viewport magnification is fractional.
- 2026-08-29T17:12Z — continuous-pinch-zoom: "still flickers during pinch drag"
  second device report. Root cause found via in-gesture frame dump (gestureLogCount
  diagnostics): mid-gesture buffer-swap base rescale used `2^(front_new − front_old)`
  instead of `2^(front_old − front_new)` — INVERTED SIGN. The frozen gesture base
  (=2^(commit − buffer)) must be DIVIDED by the level delta when the buffer
  advances: after a swap base × 2^(old − new); when the swap is the committed
  render, base collapses to exactly 1.0. Inverted sign compounded base to 252.9×
  → display showed a ~250× oversized/undersized frame → zoom jumps. Fix: flipped
  the exponent. Verify with the frame dump: after the committed buffer lands
  mid-gesture, log must show base≈1.0. Lesson: continuity transforms of the form
  scale_new = scale_old × 2^(a_old − a_new) — derive from "visual = buffer × base
  × factor invariance", don't guess the direction.
- 2026-08-29T17:30Z — continuous-pinch-zoom: "still flickers during pinch drag"
  third report. Pipeline verified CORRECT via logcat (fold base = 2^(commit−buffer)
  exact, rescale collapses to 1.0, aligned land swaps). Remaining jitter = INPUT:
  detector's cumulative zoomFactor (currDist / gestureStartDist) swings 0.6→2.1→16
  within one gesture because gestureStartDist is captured once at second-finger-down;
  with emulated pinch pointers appearing at nearly the same point the reference is
  tiny and the ratio alternates between zoom levels. Fix: (1) raise gestureStartDist
  gate 20px→60px, (2) exponential smoothing (k=0.4) on the APPLIED factor; commit
  uses the displayed (smoothed) factor so visual==commit parity is preserved.
  Lesson: verify input quality before blaming the render pipeline — an in-gesture
  frame dump showed the transform states consistent while the raw detector factor
  itself was the noise source.
- 2026-08-29T17:40Z — RESOLVED: continuous pinch flicker (all three reports) fixed
  by the combined set: (1) tile-composition dest scaling at fractional mags,
  (2) inverted-sign base rescale corrected, (3) display-hold fold at gesture end
  with base rescale maintenance, (4) gestureStartDist gate 60px + exponential
  factor smoothing (k=0.4) for emulated/noisy pinch input. User confirms pinch OK
  on emulator. Temporary in-gesture frame-dump diagnostics removed from
  MapCanvasScreen after use. Real-device pass still part of task 5.2.
